<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giengen an der Brenz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giengen an der Brenz is a town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> that grew as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> royal town and was a free imperial city from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> until 1802, when it passed to Württemberg. It adopted the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. In 1880 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margarete-steiff">Margarete Steiff</a> founded her toy business there, and the town is still the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steiff">Steiff</a> company.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giengen-an-der-brenz.jpg" alt="Giengen an der Brenz" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karlo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giengen_an_der_Brenz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Giengen lies in the district of Heidenheim at the eastern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Alb</a>, on the Brenz River about 30 kilometres north-east of Ulm, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. It stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, Ulm and Constance route, one of the main feeder routes of the Way of St. James, and walking pilgrims on their way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> pass through it. It is the home of the toy company founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margarete-steiff">Margarete Steiff</a>, who lived from 1847 to 1909.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first record of the town is a monastery chronicle of Peterhausen reporting the death in battle in 1078 of Margrave Diepold II von Vohburg, lord of Giengen. In 1147 Adele, daughter of Diepold III, married Emperor Frederick I Barbarossa; the marriage ended in divorce after a few years because it was childless. Barbarossa probably granted market rights and the unicorn coat of arms during a stay in 1171. Described as a village in 1216, Giengen seems to have been a city by 1252.</p><p>Successive emperors pawned the city more than once, and the counts of Württemberg and of Oettingen vied for control. Only in 1395 did all recognise Giengen as a free imperial city. In 1481 it was exempted from the jurisdiction of any outside court, and it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> in 1488. In the imperial register of 1521 it owed two horsemen, 13 foot soldiers and 60 gulden, near the bottom of the list of 85 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free imperial cities</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> transformed it. The first Protestant preacher, Kaspar Pfeiffelmann, preached at the hospital church in 1528, the council hired the reformer Martin Rauber in 1531, and the city officially adopted Lutheran doctrine in 1537. Catholic services were prohibited in 1556.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> brought repeated looting and ransoms by Swedish, imperial and French troops, and a fire in 1634 destroyed much of the city. The population fell from close to 2,000 in 1618 to 1,200 in 1651. The 18th century was quiet; Goethe stayed two nights at the Goldenen Gans inn in June 1788. In 1802 Duke Frederick II of Württemberg wrote that Giengen should be incorporated, and the city, with 1,695 inhabitants, lost its independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giengen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In industry, the inventions of Daimler, Maybach and Bosch made Stuttgart one of the birthplaces of the motor car and its electrical equipment, firms that still bear their founders' names, while Zeppelin's airships, Margarete Steiff's toy animals at <b>Giengen</b> and Märklin's model trains at Göppingen became widely known.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Giengen</b> grew on the river Brenz in eastern Swabia as a Hohenstaufen royal town.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-giengen">Free Imperial City of Giengen</a></li></ul></section>
